6:## What the founder decided (supersedes earlier answers where they conflict)
7:1. **Do not shrink the vision.** The full harness, agents, agentic use and autonomy stay. The reviews' concerns become
8:   design inputs, not scope cuts.
9:2. **Autonomy with the founder in the loop at the right altitude.** The system moves on its own; the founder sets direction,
10:   hears what is happening and why, and decides only what matters — never the small decisions. Find the sweet spot.
11:3. **Autonomy is a per-project switch.** 2–3 businesses/agencies (maybe one project) run autonomously; others are founder-driven.
12:4. **The system can act as a founder or as part of the company** — not only a tool the founder uses.
13:5. **It must handle anything:** startups, businesses, agencies, projects, learning, research, any idea. Most real work has no
14:   playbook. **No playbooks as the core** — they limit thinking. The system must reason, research, imagine, challenge itself,
15:   plan, execute, document, remember and improve on open-ended work.
16:6. **Claude Code and Codex are equal.** Both build, both review, both do any job. Not "Codex = reviewer only".
17:7. **Agents are identified by title and expertise, never personal names** (founder, 2026-09-30: "An engineer is a good enough title"). What matters is the role and the knowledge it carries.
18:   Agents are launched only when needed (an engineer is a Claude Code or Codex session with the right memory, context,
19:   sandbox and skills), not standing processes.
20:8. **Hybrid specialties.** Don't copy human job titles by default. AI makes it possible to fuse fields that no human could
21:   combine; design, test and compare such hybrid agents against classic roles.
22:9. **Swarms, multi-agent workflows, agents talking to each other** — use the modern patterns, deliberately.
23:10. **Memory without a graveyard.** Record what gets read and used; consolidate; forget; no write-only logs.
24:11. **Skills from scratch.** Do not anchor on the 134 curated skills. Harvest from open-source skill libraries and collections,
25:    find skills we don't yet know we need, and build an acquisition pipeline.
26:12. **Surfaces — one app we own, not outsourced:** a Mission Control centre (web/desktop) with pages for: live agents and what
27:    they are doing, monitoring their work, updates, spend and usage, tasks, an idea board / parking lot, a calendar with goals
28:    and missions, a board where dragging a mission from waiting → working → done launches the right team (Linear-like, but
29:    ours). Plus the terminal. Plus future surfaces: voice and phone calls as a reach point to the founder, and whatever comes next.
30:13. **Startup mindset plus engineering mindset** throughout: move fast, test, improve, adjust, pivot — engineered properly.
31:14. **Data-training settings** will be turned off by the founder (done by hand; the plan should still define a data policy).
